\begin{question}
\textbf{Random variables and their realizations}

An online shop records the following variables for each of its customers:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] time spent on the website (in minutes),
\item[(ii)] number of items in the shopping basket,
\item[(iii)] payment method $\in\{\texttt{credit card}, \texttt{PayPal}, \texttt{invoice}\}$,
\item[(iv)] customer's body height (in cm),
\item[(v)] whether the customer returns at least one item $\in\{\texttt{yes}, \texttt{no}\}$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{parts}
\item Decide for each variable whether it is a discrete or a continuous random variable.
\item For which of the variables does it make sense to compute a mean?
\item Which tool describes the distribution of variable (i): a density function (pdf) or a probability mass function (pmf)? Which tool describes the distribution of variable (ii)? Which tool can be used for \emph{both} (i) and (ii)?
\item Let $T_i$ denote the time that customer $i$ spends on the website. Explain the difference between $T_i$ and $T_{i,\operatorname{obs}}$. Which of the two is a fixed number?
\end{parts}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{parts}
\item Continuous: (i) time and (iv) body height; both can take any value in an interval.\\
Discrete: (ii) number of items ($\{0,1,2,\dots\}$), (iii) payment method (three possible values) and (v) returns (two possible values).
\item Only for numeric variables: (i), (ii) and (iv). The values of (iii) and (v) are categories, and the ``average payment method'' has no meaning. (If we code (v) as $1=\texttt{yes}$ and $0=\texttt{no}$, the mean of the 0/1 variable is the share of customers who return at least one item.)
\item Variable (i) is continuous, so we use a density $f_T(t)$. Variable (ii) is discrete, so we use a pmf. The cumulative distribution function $F(x)=P(X\leq x)$ works for both, since it is defined for every numeric random variable, whether continuous or discrete.
\item $T_i$ is a \emph{random variable}: before we observe customer $i$, the time spent is uncertain and could take many different values. $T_{i,\operatorname{obs}}$ is the \emph{realization}, i.e., the value we actually observed (for instance $T_{i,\operatorname{obs}}=4.3$ minutes). Only $T_{i,\operatorname{obs}}$ is a fixed number.
\end{parts}
\end{solution}

